  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe cold front passed through the region Sunday morning as\ncolder air began to infiltrate the forecast area. Lower height s\ncontinue to move through the Upper Ohio Valley as another minor\nshortwave trough also rounds the base of the longwave trough \nand into the area through the day on Sunday. This will reinforce\nthe colder air but also bring another chance of showers and \nsnow showers into the area for Sunday evening and into the \novernight.\n\nThe trough will usher in some additional cloud cover but the\ncold air advection in place will bring some subfreezing\ntemperatures to the northern forecast area for Sunday night. The\nNBM probs suggest a 70% to 90% prob of <32 degrees north of\nAllegheny county, as well as the PA and WV ridges. The Ohio\ncounties in the Freeze Watch show a more like 50% to 60% prob of\nsubfreezing temps tonight. These locations in PA, OH, and WV\nwere upgraded to a Freeze Warning. The trough passing through\ntonight will keep winds elevated and cloud cover in place so the\nchance of frost in the low lands are minimal. \n\nMonday night appears to be a near guarantee of subfreezing\ntemperatures across the entire forecast area with the lowest\nprobs at 70%. Have mentioned in the HWO that a freeze warning\nwill be needed for the entire forecast area for Monday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAfter dry days in place for Monday and Tuesday, the upper level\nridge begins to shift east as the trough over the east is\nshunted offshore. This occurs as the trough crossing the Great \nLakes brings a chance of showers and thunderstorm on Wednesday \nafternoon. The first trough will bring the potential for \nthunderstorms with a 20% to 30% chance of thunder, however, a \nnonsevere impact is likely at this time with probs keeping SB \nCAPE below 500 J/Kg. As such, the SPC agrees with this as \ngeneral thunder is currently in place. The lower atmosphere sets\nup return flow again with the surface high setting up just off \nthe southeast coast.\n\nTrends then suggest a quite Thursday with shortwave ridging\npassing the forecast area. Ensembles do hint at weak lift under\nthe ridge leading to a few stray showers on Thursday and Friday\nwith the better chance on Friday as better forcing begins to\naffect the region. Saturday still appears to be the day with a \nprominent shortwave trough beginning to impact the region. This\nstill appears to be the best day for thunderstorm potential with\nthunder probs between 25% and 35%. The NBM also provides a 30%\nto 50% probability of SB CAPE exceeding 500 J/Kg. Certainly a\nfew unresolved parameters in play to consider a higher\nconfidence but nevertheless, the thunderstorm probabilities will\nmake some impacts for important events going on during the\nweekend.",
